window.docContents[80].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/L____lambda___1___N___-___-L_lambda-___-______N-V/#Chapter5___Proposition5___22___2":{"contents":"Proposition 5.22.2. The representation L_{\\lambda + 1^N} (where 1^N = (1, 1, \\ldots, 1) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^N) is isomorphic to L_\\lambda \\otimes \\wedge^N V.\n\nProof. Indeed, L_\\lambda \\otimes \\wedge^N V \\subset V^{\\otimes n} \\otimes \\wedge^N V \\subset V^{\\otimes n+N}, and the only component of V^{\\otimes n+N} that has the same character as L_\\lambda \\otimes \\wedge^N V is L_{\\lambda + 1^N}. This implies the statement. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.22. The characters of $L_\\lambda$\tL_{lambda+1^N} ≅ L_lambda ⊗ Λ^N V","header":"L_{lambda+1^N} ≅ L_lambda ⊗ Λ^N V","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/L____lambda___1___N___-___-L_lambda-___-______N-V/#Chapter5___Proposition5___22___2"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Projective-dimension/#Chapter9___Definition9___4___1":{"contents":"Definition 9.4.1. The projective dimension \\operatorname{pd}(M) of M is the length of the shortest finite projective resolution of M (where we agree that the length of 0 \\to P_d \\to \\cdots \\to P_0 is d). If a finite projective resolution of M does not exist, then the projective dimension of M is infinite.\n\nFor instance, the projective modules are the modules of projective dimension 0.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.4. Homological dimension\tProjective dimension","header":"Projective dimension","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Projective-dimension/#Chapter9___Definition9___4___1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Bijection-between-representations-of-path-algebra-and-quiver/#Chapter2___Discussion_quiver_rep_bijection":{"contents":"We now justify our statement that a representation of a quiver is the same thing as a representation of the path algebra of a quiver.\n\nLet \\mathbf{V} be a representation of the path algebra P_Q. From this representation, we can construct a representation of Q as follows: let V_i = p_i \\mathbf{V}, and for any edge h, let x_h = a_h|_{p_{h'} \\mathbf{V}} : p_{h'} \\mathbf{V} \\longrightarrow p_{h''} \\mathbf{V} be the operator corresponding to the one-edge path h.\n\nSimilarly, let (V_i, x_h) be a representation of a quiver Q. From this representation, we can construct a representation of the path algebra P_Q: let \\mathbf{V} = \\bigoplus_i V_i, let p_i : \\mathbf{V} \\to V_i \\to \\mathbf{V} be the projection onto V_i, and for any path p = h_1 \\ldots h_m let a_p = x_{h_1} \\ldots x_{h_m} : V_{h'_m} \\to V_{h''_1} be the composition of the operators corresponding to the edges occurring in p (and the action of this operator on the other V_i is zero).\n\nIt is clear that the above assignments \\mathbf{V} \\mapsto (p_i \\mathbf{V}) and (V_i) \\mapsto \\bigoplus_i V_i are inverses of each other. Thus, we have a bijection between isomorphism classes of representations of the algebra P_Q and of the quiver Q.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.8. Quivers\tBijection between representations of path algebra and quiver","header":"Bijection between representations of path algebra and quiver","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Bijection-between-representations-of-path-algebra-and-quiver/#Chapter2___Discussion_quiver_rep_bijection"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___18___2/#Chapter5___Discussion_Theorem5___18___2_proof_end":{"contents":"This completes the proof of the theorem. \\square\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.18. Schur-Weyl duality for $\\mathfrak{gl}(V)$\tCompletion of proof of Theorem 5.18.2","header":"Completion of proof of Theorem 5.18.2","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___18___2/#Chapter5___Discussion_Theorem5___18___2_proof_end"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Section-5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___3___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.3. Frobenius divisibility\tSection 5.3: Frobenius divisibility","header":"5.3. Frobenius divisibility","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Section-5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___3___heading-1"}});
